+{"body":"## Summary\n\nAdds a checkpoint-egress trust boundary for repositories enrolled through global enable. Global enrollment may install hooks and capture sessions locally, but checkpoint data is held on the machine until the user trusts the repository once.\n\nThis is layer E of the global-enable stack:\n\n1. trail #1012 / PR #1953 — settings tier and hook gates\n2. trail #1013 / PR #1954 — lazy invisible enable\n3. trail #1014 / PR #1955 — writer, wizard, and runtime migration\n4. trail #1016 / PR #1957 — user-level agent hooks and status/doctor surfaces\n5. trail #1074 / PR #2030 — checkpoint-egress trust gate\n\n## Implementation\n\n- `settings.CheckpointEgressAllowed` gates pre-push checkpoint egress for globally enrolled repositories.\n- Explicit repo-level enable remains trusted and unchanged.\n- Trust can be granted by normalized origin identity, path identity, or machine-wide `trust_all`.\n- Interactive pushes offer one-time repo trust or machine-wide trust; non-interactive pushes hold checkpoint egress without blocking the git push.\n- `entire trust` and `entire trust --revoke` manage consent.\n- Status, doctor, session-start messaging, disable output, and security documentation expose held or untrusted state.\n- Trust checks sit above both checkpoint storage backends, so held repositories do not run the outbound checkpoint path.\n\n## Safety properties\n\n- Fail closed on malformed or unverifiable trust identity.\n- A path-trusted repository that later gains an origin must be trusted again.\n- Revocation clears both origin and path grants.\n- Existing explicitly enabled repositories retain their current behavior.\n- The trust schema ships in the same release train as layers A through D.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe current PR head passes lint, unit tests, integration shards, Windows tests, license and binary-size checks, and both deterministic canary jobs. Integration coverage exercises held push → trust → backlog drain for both storage backends.\n\nPart of #1098."}
